WEBSITE_DESCRIPTION = """Website: https://www.dds.ca.gov — California Department of Developmental Services (DDS)

The California Department of Developmental Services (DDS) is a state government agency under the California Health and Human Services Agency (CHHS). Its mission is to provide services and supports for Californians with intellectual and developmental disabilities (IDD) and their families, helping them live the life of their choosing in their communities.

Key facts about DDS:

The department is headed by Director Pete Cervinka, operates under Secretary Kim Johnson (CHHS), and under Governor Gavin Newsom. Its physical office is at 1215 O Street, Sacramento, CA 95814, with a mailing address of P.O. Box 944202, Sacramento, CA 94244-2020. Contact info: phone 833-421-0061, email info@dds.ca.gov, TTY 711.

What DDS does: DDS oversees a statewide network of 21 Regional Centers that coordinate and deliver services to individuals with developmental disabilities. Services include early intervention (Early Start program, BabyLine referrals at 800-515-2229), community-based supports, the Self-Determination Program, Home and Community-Based Services (HCBS) programs, Electronic Visit Verification (EVV), the Quality Incentive Program (QIP), and vendor/provider management. DDS also operates Ombudsperson Offices and runs Access & Equity initiatives to address service disparities.

Who it serves: Individuals with intellectual and developmental disabilities (often called "consumers" in DDS terminology) and their families across California. It also serves vendors and service providers in the developmental services system.

Key legislation: The Lanterman Developmental Disabilities Services Act (Lanterman Act) is the foundational California law governing the rights of people with developmental disabilities to services and supports.

Website sections: Individuals & Families, Services, Regional Centers, Vendors/Providers, Initiatives, Transparency (data, reports, laws/regulations), About, Career Opportunities, and an Events Calendar. The site offers content in multiple languages including Spanish, Traditional and Simplified Chinese, Tagalog, Vietnamese, and Korean.

Vision statement: People with intellectual and developmental disabilities experience respect for their culture and language preferences, their choices, beliefs, values, needs, and goals, from a person-centered service system made up of a network of community agencies that provide high quality, outcome-based, and equitable services."""
